<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    The timer PD gives other PDs a monotonic time
    and bounded timeouts. Two clients run the same program and pick their
    script by name:

        client_alpha   reads the time five times and checks it never goes
                       back; checks that malformed requests and bad timeouts
                       are refused with their typed errors; sets three
                       timeouts and cancels one at once
        client_beta    sets three timeouts, fills its fourth slot, and
                       checks that a fifth is refused

    The two clients' timeouts interleave (100, 300, 500, 700, 1100 ms; the
    cancelled one at 900 ms), so the serial output shows them expiring in
    deadline order across clients, none early, and the cancelled one never.
    The 200 ms gaps leave room for the serial output between the clients
    setting their timeouts: about 40 ms at the Pi 5's 115200 baud.

    The timer PD reads the counter and programs the CPU's EL1 physical
    timer, whose interrupt (phys_timer in the qemu_virt_aarch64 board file)
    Microkit 2.3.1 exports to user level on QEMU only. The Pi 5
    builds rpi5b_2gb/timer-demo.system instead: this system with the
    timer PD woken by BCM2712's system timer; the same expect file.

    Capabilities: the timer PD holds the timer interrupt and nothing else.
    Each client holds only its end of its channel, on which it may call the
    timer PD (pp="true") and be notified by it, and may not notify it. A
    protected procedure call must go to a strictly higher priority: the
    timer PD runs above its clients. Priorities and budgets are justified in
    timer-demo.grants.

    Every grant above is also listed in timer-demo.grants, which
    tools/check-system compares with this file and with the kernel objects
    the Microkit tool generates (make image, make qemu-test).
-->
<system>
    <protection_domain name="timer" priority="200" stack_size="0x10000">
        <program_image path="nowayos-timer.elf" />
        <irq irq="@phys_timer" id="0" trigger="@phys_timer" />
    </protection_domain>

    <protection_domain name="client_alpha" priority="100" stack_size="0x10000">
        <program_image path="nowayos-timer-client.elf" />
    </protection_domain>

    <protection_domain name="client_beta" priority="90" stack_size="0x10000">
        <program_image path="nowayos-timer-client.elf" />
    </protection_domain>

    <!-- Each client calls the timer; the timer notifies it of expiries. -->
    <channel>
        <end pd="client_alpha" id="0" pp="true" notify="false" />
        <end pd="timer" id="1" />
    </channel>
    <channel>
        <end pd="client_beta" id="0" pp="true" notify="false" />
        <end pd="timer" id="2" />
    </channel>
</system>
